WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.274
An integral accumulates small contributions

00:00:02.274 --> 00:00:08.645
If a rate stays at three units per second for two seconds, the total is six units

00:00:08.645 --> 00:00:14.267
The graph shows a rectangle whose height is rate and whose width is elapsed time.

00:00:15.567 --> 00:00:21.606
When the rate changes, narrow rectangles approximate the accumulation, and a limit gives the exact integral

00:00:21.606 --> 00:00:29.533
For a rate equal to time between zero and two, the region is a triangle with base two and height two.

00:00:30.833 --> 00:00:32.849
Integrals count signed accumulation

00:00:32.849 --> 00:00:37.887
This velocity is negative before time one and positive afterward

00:00:37.887 --> 00:00:40.910
Equal areas cancel, giving zero displacement

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:48.467
Total distance instead adds their magnitudes, producing one unit rather than zero for this journey.

00:00:49.767 --> 00:00:52.323
An indefinite integral describes all antiderivatives

00:00:52.323 --> 00:00:58.715
Every vertical shift of x squared has derivative two x, so a constant must remain

00:00:58.715 --> 00:01:05.533
A definite integral is a number between bounds; an indefinite integral is a family of functions.
